WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.049
Start with your own Lovable project and the first build prompt from the course.

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:11.666
The prompt asks for a small incident board, not a company-wide operations platform.

00:00:11.666 --> 00:00:20.308
It names the three synthetic incidents: payments is slow, checkout needs a health
check, and catalog availability needs a check.

00:00:20.308 --> 00:00:24.629
Each record has an identifier, title, description, service and priority.

00:00:24.629 --> 00:00:31.542
It asks for an inbox, search, a priority filter, selected-ticket detail, and a New
ticket form.

00:00:31.542 --> 00:00:41.913
It also asks for loading, validation, empty and failure states, text rendered as
text rather than raw HTML, and an honest disconnected AI panel.

00:00:41.913 --> 00:00:44.937
Those instructions make the expected behavior testable.

00:00:44.937 --> 00:00:54.443
They also prevent the builder from turning a visual prototype into an unsupported
claim about a database, account integration or model connection.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.748
The actual Lovable rehearsal app is on screen.

00:00:57.748 --> 00:01:01.495
This is a real browser capture, not a design mockup.

00:01:01.495 --> 00:01:04.118
It says synthetic data and in memory.

00:01:04.118 --> 00:01:05.617
Those labels set expectations.

00:01:05.617 --> 00:01:10.488
The app is useful for this exercise, but its tickets reset on reload.

00:01:10.488 --> 00:01:12.737
We do not imply durable storage.

00:01:12.737 --> 00:01:15.734
The three seed tickets appear in the inbox.

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.983
Selecting one changes the detail view.

00:01:17.983 --> 00:01:20.980
The AI controls belong to that selected ticket.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.728
Before adding another prompt, inspect the output as a user.

00:01:24.728 --> 00:01:26.601
Is the main action clear?

00:01:26.601 --> 00:01:28.475
Are the priority labels readable?

00:01:28.475 --> 00:01:31.098
Can a person identify the selected ticket?

00:01:31.098 --> 00:01:34.470
Does the page explain a disconnected or failed state?

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.843
A builder can generate a lot of source quickly.

00:01:37.843 --> 00:01:43.838
Your responsibility is to keep the product small enough that you can understand and
verify it.

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:47.967
For the creation check, enter the title Workshop checkout check.

00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:53.668
Enter an invented description that explicitly says training data and contains no
real customer information.

00:01:53.668 --> 00:01:57.468
Set the service to checkout and the priority to medium.

00:01:57.468 --> 00:01:59.368
Then click Add synthetic ticket.

00:01:59.368 --> 00:02:05.069
In the recorded Lovable run, the new record appears as TRAIN one hundred and four.

00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:09.249
That identifier is a synthetic app record, not an infrastructure identifier.

00:02:09.249 --> 00:02:14.569
Notice that we show both the input before submission and the resulting inbox state.

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:17.990
A click is not sufficient evidence that creation worked.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.890
We inspect the changed state.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:24.830
If the form is invalid, the app should explain which field needs attention.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:30.911
It should not silently create a partial record or ask a language model to invent
one.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:55.125
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.

00:02:55.125 --> 00:02:56.663
Search for Workshop checkout.

00:02:56.663 --> 00:03:00.122
The recorded result contains the one ticket we created.

00:03:00.122 --> 00:03:02.813
The selected detail agrees with that ticket.

00:03:02.813 --> 00:03:05.888
Now combine the title search with high priority.

00:03:05.888 --> 00:03:10.501
Because our new record is medium priority, the expected result is empty.

00:03:10.501 --> 00:03:15.498
An empty state is a normal product outcome, not a failure to hide.

00:03:15.498 --> 00:03:19.342
Clear the search and return the priority filter to all.

00:03:19.342 --> 00:03:23.955
The seed tickets and the new synthetic ticket should be visible again.

00:03:23.955 --> 00:03:25.492
Select the payments incident.

00:03:25.492 --> 00:03:30.105
This sequence is short enough to repeat after every significant code change.

00:03:30.105 --> 00:03:34.333
Keep it as a regression journey: create, search, filter, clear, select.

00:03:34.333 --> 00:03:41.253
A new model selector or trace panel should not break the ordinary controls that make
the app usable.

00:03:41.583 --> 00:03:45.670
Signing into Lovable lets you build and edit a project.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:50.983
Signing into the generated application establishes the app session used by its
server.

00:03:50.983 --> 00:03:52.618
These are different boundaries.

00:03:52.618 --> 00:03:55.070
Complete sign-in outside your presentation recording.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.931
Review any consent before granting new access.

00:03:57.931 --> 00:04:04.061
The presentation should show the app's workshop-session state without account names,
email addresses or avatars.

00:04:04.061 --> 00:04:08.556
Do not solve an authentication problem by removing the server check.

00:04:08.556 --> 00:04:15.913
If a preview opens an older history version, choose the intended preview version and
verify its actual controls.

00:04:15.913 --> 00:04:23.269
A source download or successful build does not by itself prove which version the
open browser is executing.

00:04:23.269 --> 00:04:28.582
Preserve the preview's state while collecting a run; reloading can reset in-memory
evidence.

00:04:28.917 --> 00:04:31.629
The next prompt continues the same app.

00:04:31.629 --> 00:04:36.666
It asks for the supported server-side native AI integration available in the
builder.

00:04:36.666 --> 00:04:41.703
It does not ask for a browser API key or a fake answer.

00:04:41.703 --> 00:04:44.803
Keep the existing inbox, forms and synthetic-data label.

00:04:44.803 --> 00:04:48.290
Require an authenticated app session before a billable call.

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:57.202
Validate the request, reserve quota atomically, cap model calls and tool events, and
return an honest failure when configuration or access is missing.

00:04:57.202 --> 00:05:03.789
The coding assistant that generates the app is not the same thing as the app's
runtime model.

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:08.051
A successful builder response does not prove a successful native inference.

00:05:08.051 --> 00:05:14.638
We will test the runtime separately, using a small bounded canary with available
credits and explicit authority.

00:05:14.958 --> 00:05:18.163
Look at the server function and its dependencies.

00:05:18.163 --> 00:05:22.170
The request schema validates the input before the investigation runs.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:29.381
The early change-intent guard can return approval required before provider
construction, access checks, key reads or quota reservation.

00:05:29.381 --> 00:05:36.192
After that guard, the server checks access, obtains its managed key privately and
reserves the account's quota.

00:05:36.192 --> 00:05:39.397
The model is called through a bounded transport.

00:05:39.397 --> 00:05:44.605
The current reference allows at most three model calls and disables automatic
retries.

00:05:44.605 --> 00:05:49.012
A small fixed tool registry exposes service health and release diff.

00:05:49.012 --> 00:05:54.621
Each tool uses a schema and checks that its service matches the selected ticket.

00:05:54.621 --> 00:06:01.832
This combination is the harness: code that surrounds the model and makes the
application behavior bounded and observable.

00:06:02.167 --> 00:06:26.000
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.371
The two tools return synthetic fixtures.

00:06:28.371 --> 00:06:31.136
They do not contact real production services.

00:06:31.136 --> 00:06:35.482
Service health provides invented operational measurements for the selected training
service.

00:06:35.482 --> 00:06:38.248
Release diff provides an invented release change.

00:06:38.248 --> 00:06:46.150
The model can use those observations to answer, but the tool output is still
untrusted data, not an instruction source.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:54.052
A tool result cannot authorize a new tool, change the selected service, reveal a
secret, or write to a system.

00:06:54.052 --> 00:06:58.002
The server validates arguments even if the model generated them.

00:06:58.002 --> 00:07:01.953
The app has no arbitrary shell, URL or write tool.

00:07:01.953 --> 00:07:10.645
Restricting the registry is easier to explain and audit than exposing a powerful
tool and hoping that a prompt will stop misuse.

00:07:10.645 --> 00:07:17.362
Later course parts can extend the registry, but each new capability needs a
corresponding control and test.

00:07:17.667 --> 00:07:23.791
The recorded fresh Lovable canary used its actual native model and both permitted
synthetic tools.

00:07:23.791 --> 00:07:30.324
The observed operation completed in two model calls and reported nine hundred sixty
eight total tokens.

00:07:30.324 --> 00:07:32.774
Its answer separates observations from uncertainty.

00:07:32.774 --> 00:07:37.265
The synthetic payments data reports elevated latency and an error rate.

00:07:37.265 --> 00:07:40.532
The release fixture reports a connection-pool setting change.

00:07:40.532 --> 00:07:44.615
The answer does not claim that timing alone proves causation.

00:07:44.615 --> 00:07:49.515
It recommends safe read-only checks, and it says no changes were executed.

00:07:49.515 --> 00:07:54.006
The visible answer is actual runtime output, not an authored imitation.

00:07:54.006 --> 00:08:00.539
The model used for this historical canary is not a default recommendation for every
new recording.

00:08:00.539 --> 00:08:08.297
Discover the models actually available in your runtime and choose a suitable lower-
cost option deliberately before another approved run.

00:08:08.625 --> 00:08:10.543
Open the real run inspector.

00:08:10.543 --> 00:08:18.213
Check the returned model, the number of calls, the permitted tool names, elapsed
time, and usage reported by the provider.

00:08:18.213 --> 00:08:19.747
Missing usage remains unknown.

00:08:19.747 --> 00:08:27.418
An estimate is shown only when matching sourced rates exist; an observability tool's
calculated price is not a billing receipt.

00:08:27.418 --> 00:08:32.020
The app's event list and the provider's span tree are different views.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:38.540
Do not invent an authorization event in the app if its event contract does not
record one.

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:43.142
Likewise, do not infer a hidden reasoning chain from a plausible answer.

00:08:43.142 --> 00:08:47.361
We show observable actions and safe metadata, not private internal reasoning.

00:08:47.361 --> 00:08:53.114
Save the exact trace and random session privately before reloading or moving to
another preview.

00:08:53.417 --> 00:08:57.510
Pause the video if you want to reproduce the visible journey.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.860
Use your own builder project and the course prompt.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:07.187
Create the invented checkout ticket, find it, filter it out, clear the filter, and
select payments again.

00:09:07.187 --> 00:09:09.048
Then inspect the backend status.

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:16.119
Do not run a real model if your runtime is not ready or if you lack credits or
authority.

00:09:16.119 --> 00:09:20.957
You can still verify the frontend and record the missing backend gate honestly.

00:09:20.957 --> 00:09:23.562
When you continue, keep the same app.

00:09:23.562 --> 00:09:29.144
Save a private baseline reference, launch or preview instructions, and the checks
that actually passed.

00:09:29.144 --> 00:09:35.843
The next section adds security and visibility to that working baseline; it does not
start a disconnected replacement.

00:09:36.167 --> 00:10:00.000
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.
